{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CghQdicDJbeU39xgm5Cqu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The ligand-field model cannot give Dq or B for this species, so I am not reporting numbers.\n\nVanadate, VO₄³⁻, contains vanadium(V), which is a d⁰ ion. With no d electrons there are no d–d transitions, so the ligand-field splitting (Dq) and the Racah parameters (B, C) have no spectroscopic content here. The three intense bands (ε of roughly 2 700–9 600 M⁻¹ cm⁻¹, far above the typical d–d range of about 10–100 M⁻¹ cm⁻¹) are ligand-to-metal charge-transfer bands, O(2p) → V(3d). In tetrahedral symmetry these arise from transitions out of the filled oxygen-based orbitals into the empty V 3d-derived t₂ and e levels. Their energies depend on the donor–acceptor electronegativity and bonding, not on Dq or on interelectron repulsion. Fitting them to a d-electron ligand-field model would give meaningless values.\n\nDq and B are therefore undefined for VO₄³⁻ in this model. A meaningful analysis would need a d¹ or higher vanadium complex, such as V(IV) in VO²⁺ or V(III) in [V(H₂O)₆]³⁺, whose d–d bands can be assigned.\n\n{\"Dq\": null, \"B\": null, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": true, \"reason\": \"VO4^3- is a d0 V(V) complex whose bands are O→V charge-transfer, not d–d transitions, so Dq and B cannot be defined within the ligand-field model.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":253,"cache_read_input_tokens":627,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":253,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f91ee472-ed10-479e-9cd8-8e83e3501545","uuid":"f9cf2fd5-1fd8-4975-80f1-5b90e7532ab3","timestamp":"2026-11-04T06:23:38.827Z","requ